{title: A Mighty Fortress Is Our God}
{artist: Martin Luther · tr. Frederick Hedge}
{key: C}
{time: 4/4}
{tempo: 92}

Verse 1
A [C]mighty fortress [G]is our [C]God, a [F]bulwark [C]never [G]fail[C]ing;
our helper he a[G]mid the [C]flood of [F]mortal [C]ills pre[G]vail[C]ing.
For [G]still our ancient [Am]foe doth [Em]seek to [F]work us [G]woe —
his [C]craft and power are [Am]great, and, [F]armed with cruel [C]hate,
on [F]earth is [C]not his [G]e[C]qual.

Verse 2
Did [C]we in [G]our own strength [C]confide, our [F]striving [C]would [G]be
losing; Were not the right Man [C]on our [F]side, [C]the Man
of [G]God's own choosing: [Am]Dost ask [Em]who that may [F]be? Christ [G]Jesus, it
is [C]He; Lord Sabaoth, His [Am]name, from age [F]to age the [C]same,
And [F]He must [C]win the [G]battle.

Verse 3
And [C]though this [G]world, with devils [C]filled, should [F]threaten [C]to [G]undo
us, We will not fear, for [C]God hath [F]willed [C]His truth
to [G]triumph through us: [Am]The Prince [Em]of Darkness grim, [F]we tremble [G]not for
him; [C]His rage we can [Am]endure, for lo, [F]his doom is [C]sure,
One [F]little word [C]shall fell [G]him.

Verse 4
That [C]word above [G]all earthly [C]pow'rs, no [F]thanks [C]to [G]them,
abideth; The Spirit and [C]the gifts [F]are [C]ours through
Him [G]who with us [Am]sideth: Let [Em]goods [F]and kindred [G]go, this
mortal [C]life also; The body [Am]they [F]may kill: God's [C]truth
abideth [F]still, His [C]kingdom is [G]forever.
